# Use existing traces first

When the user reports a JS / SIM / FPGA runtime bug (READY with no error,
TRAP, wrong screen, etc.):

1. **Look at the newest files under `traces/`** (especially `*_FPGA-SIM.log`
   and the matching host `.log`) before writing or running any repro script.
2. Start at the **end of the trace**: fault / trap / note / typed lines, then
   any µop / flight tail before the fault.
3. Prefer diagnosis from that evidence. **Do not re-run heavy demos or the GUI**
   to reproduce unless the user explicitly asks, or the trace is
   missing/incomplete.
4. Offline helpers that only read sources, token streams, microcode listings,
   or the existing log are fine; full sim / GUI repro is not the default.
5. If you must run something later, say why the trace was not enough first.

## Feature order before board proof

User-visible behaviour must already exist on **PYTHON bytecode VM**, then
**FPGA-SIM RTL**, before treating the board as the place that “defines” it.
See `python-first-parity` and `no-dukpy-cheat-native-cpu`. Chrome/dukpy
looking good is **not** PYTHON proof. A board-only win with GUI/SIM reject
is a **parity gap**, not a successful ship.

## Verification must include the real board

A fix that claims silicon is not verified until it runs on the **FPGA
hardware**. FPGA-SIM and the Python **bytecode** FM are necessary but not
sufficient. Always confirm on the board **after** PYTHON + FPGA-SIM match,
never sim alone and never board-only UX. ASIC only after FPGA honesty.

## This product is not the BASIC machine

Do not assume Nexys A7-100T pinouts, BASIC tokens, or BASIC LUT-shrink history.
Debug *this* repo’s traces and RTL.
